+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>...
+
+    Versions to sort. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard input*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text, one
+version per line, in ascending semver order. This makes the output
+easy to consume in a shell loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 | while read v; do echo "release $v"; done
+    release 1.2.0
+    release 1.10.0
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If any argument (or input line) is not a valid version, nothing is printed on standard
+out and the return code is not zero.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Find the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>...
+
+    Versions to search. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard input*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed on *standard out* as a JSON object, so it can be
+parsed by other tools::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 1.10.0 2.0.0-rc.1
+    {"version": "1.10.0"}
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver latest
+    {"version": "1.10.0"}
+
+If any version is invalid, or none of them is without a prerelease part,
+nothing is printed on standard out and the return code is not zero.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
